        "subject": "LU-20856 osc: send at most one reclaim sync per target",
        "message": "LU-20856 osc: send at most one reclaim sync per target\n\nWriteback started by memory reclaim forces a commit for each file\nit visits. One reclaim in a memory-limited cgroup starts such a\npass over the dirty files of every cgroup on the client, and each\nfile sent its own OST_SYNC for each of its stripes whenever the\ntarget had any uncommitted pages at all. A client writing from\nseveral small cgroups sent hundreds of OST_SYNC a second, each a\nforced journal commit on the target.\n\nSyncing an object commits every write the target did up to that\nobject's last one, so one sync covers the other files' earlier\nwrites as well. Track the transno of the latest bulk write reply\nper object, and per target the write covered by the reclaim sync\nlast sent. Send a reclaim sync only for an object with an\nuncommitted write, and only when no earlier reclaim sync to that\ntarget is still waiting for its commit. If the reply shows the\ntarget did not commit that far, release the claim so that a later\npass can send another. Short I/O writes pin no pages and are not\ntracked. Data-on-MDT objects get the same gate.\n\nAn object refused while a sync is in flight, whose write that sync\ndoes not cover, is not revisited until it writes again: its pages\nwait for the target's own commit or a later reclaim sync to that\ntarget.\n\nWith llite.*.unstable_stats off, the old test never saw uncommitted\npages, so reclaim now also syncs objects it used to skip.\n\nWith 4 cgroups of 4 writers on one OST and memory.max 256M, this\ncuts OST_SYNC from about 700 to about 100 per 12 GB written, with\nno change in throughput and no OOM.\n\nFixes: 8aa231a99468 (\"LU-16713 llite: writeback/commit pages under memory pressure\")\nAssisted-by: ClaudeCode:claude-opus-5-5\nSigned-off-by: Patrick Farrell <patrick@thelustrecollective.com>\nChange-Id: Ie57ecb5b75066c648f3fc257f8461eb71ec4b78a\n"
      },
